      "section_breaks": [
        {
          "before": "these are familiar interference patterns.\nIn the passage quoted by the textbook, Mark Twain describes soap bubbles: \"Soap bubbles floating through the air ... sparkle with the magnificent colors of the objects around them. A soap bubble is perhaps nature's most wonderful and most delicate miracle.\"\nLight interference is an important reason why soap bubbles look so colorful.",
          "after": "The textbook credits the British scientist Thomas Young with an important early explanation of the colors of thin oil films, describing him as the first to reach this ingenious conclusion.\nHis explanation was that wave $1$ and wave $2$ combine where they meet, producing different colors in the reflected light."
        },
        {
          "before": "Within this model and total-phase convention, if wave $2$, which enters and returns from the film, has an effective delay relative to reflected wave $1$ equal to an integer number of wavelengths, their oscillations act together and the light is **reinforced**.\nIf the second wave's effective delay is half a wavelength, or an odd number of half-wavelengths, their oscillations oppose each other and the light is **weakened**.\nThis is not a geometric-distance rule that can be applied directly to every oil film: first determine whether reflection reverses the phase at each interface, using the media on either side of the film, and then compare the waves' total phases.",
          "after": "For a stable interference pattern to form, the two waves must be **coherent**.\nFor the same monochromatic light compared in the same medium, this means that the waves have the same wavelength and a constant phase difference at the same observation point."
        },
        {
          "before": "Two ordinary independent light sources do not share this phase relationship, and the phase difference between their waves changes continually.\nOver an ordinary observation time, the changing reinforcement and weakening average out, so no stable interference pattern is visible.",
          "after": "The textbook also explains that Young recognized that **differences in light's color are related to differences in wavelength or frequency**.\nWhen compared in the same medium, monochromatic visible light of different wavelengths corresponds to different colors, while frequency describes how quickly the light's oscillation cycles repeat."
        },
        {
          "before": "A single part of a film can therefore reinforce some reflected colors while weakening others, rather than making every color brighter or darker in the same way.\nIf the thickness differs from one part of the film to another, the combination of reinforced and weakened colors differs too.\nThis is why a film of uneven thickness displays different colors under white light.",
          "after": "Knowing that color is related to light's frequency and wavelength lets us understand dispersion more deeply than in the previous unit.\nWe must consider not only which color the light appears to be, but also how its refractive index in a medium depends on these physical properties."
        },
        {
          "before": "When describing this dependence using wavelength, specify whether the comparison uses wavelengths in vacuum or in a particular medium;\na wavelength change on entering another medium does not mean that the light's color has changed.\nA refractive index that varies with frequency can cause different colors to bend by different amounts during refraction, linking the separation of colors in the previous unit to the physical properties of light waves.",
          "after": "In its account of scientific history, the textbook describes Thomas Young (1773-1829) as an outstanding British scientist with unusually broad interests and many talents.\nHe was a well-known physician, an insightful physicist and astronomer, and also worked in mechanical engineering and metallurgy."
        }
      ],
